@@ -78,6 +78,48 @@ a FIFO. The daemon is spawned with `PULSE_SERVER`/`PULSE_SINK` pointing at its s
 
 ## Volume modes
 
+The provider's `volume_mode` option defaults to `player_only`. Existing saved options
+and defaults are unchanged; the additional option does not require a data migration.
+
+### Spotify-authoritative volume (opt-in)
+
+Set `volume_mode` to **`spotify_authoritative`** ("Spotify app controls volume").
+Music Assistant never writes the Spotify slider in this mode, including at device
+activation, source selection, receiver-volume adoption, or a local MA volume change.
+Receiver reports are cached before the source is claimed. Before activation they
+are candidates only: inactive or unconfirmed snapshots cannot authorize playback.
+Device activation requests a fresh `get_state` snapshot; a snapshot explicitly
+reporting `is_active: true` with a volume confirms the current receiver session.
+An explicit active snapshot may also arrive before the device event. Early level
+reports are never promoted merely because a later device event says active; MA
+waits for this confirmation without requiring another user volume gesture.
+There is no player-wins
+startup grace period or echo-value filter. Before serving a source, MA commands the
+receiver's reported level on the consuming player and checks its reported logical
+volume (or group volume). Unknown volume, failed commands, or a mismatched readback
+refuse source selection rather than falling back to the restored player level.
+
+The capture sink starts muted. On each volume report it is muted while the player
+adopts the level, then uses the same cubic reciprocal compensation as `sync_spotify`.
+Failure leaves it muted and invokes the existing capture recovery. Pause retains
+the session volume. Device/session loss, daemon loss, and control-websocket loss
+invalidate it. Auth reports expose no account identifier, so each auth report also
+invalidates the cached level and requests a fresh playback snapshot when active.
+Volume-before-claim and playing-before-device events are supported; playback waits
+for a level. A local MA volume change does not write the receiver and the next
+receiver report/source selection adopts the receiver level again.
+
+This is a volume ownership policy, **not a guarantee against transition artifacts**:
+the receiver can change PCM attenuation before its asynchronous volume event arrives,
+while the old reciprocal sink gain is still applied. Muting during adoption cannot
+eliminate that earlier window. The existing `sync_spotify` artifact warning also
+applies here. A fresh receiver snapshot is current receiver state, not proof that a
+particular human chose that value. Tests use isolated fakes and check static gain
+algebra and ordering; they do not qualify real Soloist audio, hardware volume readback,
+or account-transfer behavior. These require separate receiver/capture qualification.
+
+### Existing modes
+
 - **`player_only` (default, quality-first)**: the daemon is pinned at 100% so unity-gain
   PCM reaches the FIFO; the MA player owns the audible volume. Off-100 reports from the
   daemon (user dragging the app slider) re-pin it; a failed pin marks the cached volume
